// mgeHost64 — the PER-VIEW sky cbuffer (tasks/forge-atmosphere.md S2c; was forge-physical-sky.md P2).
//
// ⚠ THIS FILE DECLARES A STRUCT AND NOTHING ELSE — NO SRT. It is included by opaque.srt.h, which
// puts gSkyView on the PerBatch set for skyhw.frag. Two SRTs in one .srt.h alias each other's
// registers and it compiles clean because DXC dead-strips the unused one, so a shared cbuffer STRUCT
// lives in its own header — a struct carries no registers, so sharing it is free.
// [[project_forge_srt_one_per_header]]
//
// ⚠ S2 EMPTIED THE BIG HALF OF THIS STRUCT, AND THE REASON IT STILL EXISTS IS UNCHANGED. It used to
// carry 36 floats of cooked Hosek-Wilkie coefficients, because a closed form has to be handed its
// configuration. The atmosphere is a LUT — the sky pass samples gAtmosSkyView and needs no
// coefficients at all — so those nine float4s are gone. What survives is the ONE field this cbuffer
// was created for and which nothing else can supply: THIS view's invViewProj. See below.
//
// ─── WHY A SECOND CBUFFER EXISTS AT ALL, WHEN gShadowParams IS RIGHT THERE ────────────────────────
// Because of ONE field: invViewProj. gShadowParams already carries the main camera's, and it is
// bound BY POINTER into every PerFrame set — which is the property that let P1's anchor reach the
// mirror for free, and is exactly what makes it useless here. The mirror sky pass binds the SAME
// buffer, so a fullscreen pass reconstructing rays from gShadowParams.invViewProj in the reflect
// pass would reconstruct MAIN-VIEW rays and paint the main view's sky into the water. The sky was
// geometry until now, so it inherited its view from the vertex stage and this never came up.
//
// TWO INSTANCES, main and reflect, bound through the pPerBatchSetSky / pPerBatchSetReflectSky pair
// the sky pass already switches between — which is that same one-resource-two-views pattern, already
// proven by the sky world windows riding it.
//
// The remaining scalar lanes are IDENTICAL in both instances (a radiance scale and a moon's level do
// not depend on which camera is looking), and they ride here anyway rather than in gShadowParams so
// that everything the sky pass needs arrives through one binding and the mirror cannot be handed
// half of it.
#pragma once

// The view that reads the atmosphere, its unit conversion, and (P2b) the sky ELEMENTS whose radiance
// the model sets rather than MW. 36 floats == 144 B; the CBV is 512 B.
STRUCT(SkyViewData)
{
    // THIS view's inverse viewProj. Reverse-Z, camera-relative, half-pixel-adjusted — i.e. exactly
    // the matrix that view rasterises with, inverted, so a difference of two reconstructed points is
    // the true world-space ray direction for this view (and for the mirror, the REFLECTED ray).
    DATA(float4x4, invViewProj, None);
    // xyz = SPARE. This held the model's per-channel radiance term; the medium states its own level.
    // w   = NATIVE -> SCENE scale, with the sky strength already folded in. Native is
    //       W/(m^2 sr) per linear sRGB primary; scene is what the rest of the renderer works in. One
    //       lane, folded host-side, so no shader ever holds a piece of the conversion.
    //       ⚠ 0 IS A LEGITIMATE VALUE HERE (interior, feature off) and the pass must survive it as
    //       "black sky", NOT as a divide or a guard — unlike calParams.z in P1, which was a
    //       multiplier whose 0 meant "the binding failed".
    //       ⚠ AND THE NIGHT RAMP IS **NOT** IN IT ANY MORE (S2e). The ramp used to fade the drawn
    //       sky to black because Hosek-Wilkie was undefined below the horizon; a medium computes
    //       twilight instead of fading it, so the SKY half of the ramp's two jobs is retired here.
    //       Its LIGHTING half — handing the physical blend back to MW's authored night, which
    //       calTarget()'s night row is calibrated against — survives untouched, host-side.
    DATA(float4, radiance, None);
    // xyz = unit vector TOWARD the sun, world space, MW Z up. The model is parameterised by the
    //       angle to the sun, so this is not lighting data — it is a coordinate.
    //       ⚠ gFrameData.sunDir is MW's TRAVEL direction and is the NEGATIVE of this. Converted once
    //       host-side rather than negated at each use.
    // w   = the CAMERA'S RADIUS in the medium's own coordinates (metres from the planet centre).
    //       ⚠ THE SKY PASS NEEDS THIS AND CANNOT DERIVE IT. The sky-view LUT is parameterised at the
    //       altitude it was cooked for, so deparameterising a direction into a uv needs the same
    //       radius the cook used — and the frag has no world position, only a direction. It rides
    //       here rather than being recomputed from an eye height that the park restamp is allowed to
    //       move ([[project_park_restamp_eye_origin]]).
    DATA(float4, sunDirW, None);
    // x = 1 while the pass is armed (exterior, model cooked), 0 otherwise. A hard gate rather than a
    //     zero radiance, so "the sky is legitimately black" and "nothing was published" stay
    //     distinguishable in a capture.
    // yzw spare.
    DATA(float4, params, None);
    // ─── P2b: THE SUN DISC, AS RADIANCE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // xyz = L_sunDisc, the disc's PEAK radiance in SCENE units, per channel: E_normal / Omega_sprite,
    //       where E_normal is the model's own direct-normal solar irradiance and Omega_sprite is the
    //       solid angle MW'S QUAD actually covers — computed host-side from the draw's world
    //       transform and the mesh's model-space bound, and PRINTED, because it is NOT the sun's
    //       6.8e-5 sr. MW's is a big glare sprite; the ratio is a finding, not a constant.
    //       Per CHANNEL so the disc reddens at sunset the way the transmittance says it should —
    //       which, with the energy tracking E_normal, is what makes it dim at dusk and bright at
    //       noon BY ITSELF. That self-regulation is the whole reason this is physical and not pinned.
    // w   = the disc's OWN whiteness-falloff exponent for expandExposedEmissiveP(). ⚠ NOT
    //       gShadowParams.skyAO2.w: that lane is the material-emissive path's and this is a glare
    //       sprite; sharing it would make one slider move two unrelated things. 0 = exact bypass,
    //       which is a UNIFORM gain and renders the sprite as a bright SQUARE
    //       ([[project_sdr_exposed_not_radiance]]) — it is a diagnostic setting, not a default.
    DATA(float4, sunDisc, None);
    // ─── P2b: THE TWO ELEMENTS WHOSE LEVEL THE MODEL SETS RATHER THAN MW ──────────────────────────
    // x = THE STAR LAYER's radiance in SCENE units. Stars are drawn ADDITIVELY over the physical sky
    //     (see forgerender.cpp's sky loop), so this ONE number decides day and night at once and no
    //     fade curve is involved: by day the sky under them drowns them, by night the physical sky
    //     has ramped to black and the same radiance is all there is, so the servo's night exposure
    //     brings it up. That asymmetry is the camera behaviour the user asked for ("stars can expose
    //     up instead of fade"), and it is why the space between them goes correctly black.
    //     ⚠ IT DOES **NOT** RIDE gSkyHwStrength, unlike everything else here. That lane is the
    //     ATMOSPHERE's A/B level; a star is not atmosphere, and coupling them made the tuned value
    //     depend on where an unrelated slider happened to sit.
    // y = THE CLOUD LAYER's radiance for a fully-lit (authored 1.0) texel, SCENE units:
    //     albedo_cloud * E_total/pi, with the night ramp and the sky strength folded in.
    //     ⚠ THIS IS AN ANCHOR, NOT A DIM. MW's cloud layer is authored display data, and P2 dropped
    //     the sky ~1.1 stops beneath it without touching it — so the clouds kept riding the exposure
    //     on their own. At night that is severe: the sky is black, the servo runs E = 14-27 to meter
    //     a dark ground, and an authored cloud at ~0.4 linear lands near display 240. Reported in
    //     play, 2026-08-21: *"clouds are a lot brighter compared to MW."* Deriving the level from
    //     the light the frame is actually casting fixes both ends at once — the ratio to the zenith
    //     is albedo/q (~6.4x at the reference, the physical band), and at night the ramp takes it to
    //     0 so the clouds go dark WITH the sky while still occluding the stars through their alpha.
    //     ⚠ S2j: WITH skyCloudBeam > 0 THIS IS ONLY THE DIFFUSE-SKY PART, albedo * E_sky/pi (+ the
    //     night floor); the direct beam moved to z/w and is evaluated per channel at the deck. With
    //     skyCloudBeam = 0 it is the full E_total anchor above, to the bit.
    // z = albedo/pi * native->scene scale, for the BEAM (sky.frag multiplies it by atmosSunBeamAt()).
    //     0 = beam off (skyCloudBeam = 0, or no physical sky) and sky.frag skips the lookup.
    // w = the radius the beam is taken at: the cloud deck's BASE, metres from the planet centre
    //     (Rg + cloudBottomKm, 1.5 km when a weather states none).
    DATA(float4, elemRadiance, None);
};
